# Figures for the final comparison with exploration (epsilon = 0.02) and for what
# tile coding and RBF learned about each state variable (from the saved weights).
#
#   python3 tools/plot_final.py [--results results] [--out results/figures] [--ieee]
#
# Inputs (under --results; the newest file per prefix is used):
#   cmp-{tc,rbf,nn}-final_*.csv, cmp-{tc,rbf,nn}-reduced-final_*.csv
#       epsilon = 0.02 training runs, searched in results/ and results/after-code-merge/
#   cmp-{tc,rbf,nn}-sarsa-final_*.csv  the same runs with Sarsa (fig17)
#   cmp-nn-*-greedy-eval_*.csv      greedy evaluation of the saved network weights
#   precode-merge-results/cmp-*-final_*.csv   the greedy (epsilon = 0) final runs
#   prev runs/benchmarks_*.csv      hand-coded / random / always-hold benchmarks
#   weights/cmp-{tc,rbf}[-reduced]-final/seed-*.txt   weights written by save()
#
# How the per-variable curves are computed:
#   Both approximators use one-variable tilings / one-variable Gaussians, so
#   Q(s, a) = sum over state variables i of f_i,a(x_i). For tile coding f_i,a(x) is
#   the sum of the 10 active tile weights of variable i; for RBF it is the
#   Gaussian-weighted sum of variable i's 200 centre weights (sigma = 0.01).
#   Only the shape of f_i,a is meaningful: a constant can move from one variable to
#   another without changing Q, so each curve is shifted to mean zero over the
#   values that were visited. A value counts as visited when every seed updated
#   the weights there (weights start at exactly 0 and stay 0 until updated).
#   Visited values are weighted equally; the weights do not record how often a
#   value occurred.
#
# Output (in --out): fig10..fig17 PNGs and summary_final.csv
